From a7f660c4b060c3371663506810468b3cf65028ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirill Mitrofanov <kmitrqq@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:29:53 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_adsp: add X1E80100 ADSP and CDSP
 for Linux at EL2

When Linux runs at EL2 on X1E80100 there is no hypervisor (QHEE) to set
the DSPs up around TZ PAS, and TZ alone cannot start them. Start them the
way this driver already does on SDM845 and SC7280: Linux copies the images
into the carveouts and releases the core through the QDSP6SS boot FSM.

What X1E80100 needs on top of that, all of which QHEE does otherwise:

- A second image. Each DSP also runs a DTB image from its own carveout,
  and finds it through a descriptor at qdsp6ss+0x60..0x74 that a reset
  clears. The descriptor also carries the chip and platform identity the
  DSP uses to pick its configuration from that image; take it from
  socinfo and pack it the way QHEE does. Without the descriptor a DSP
  still boots, but without that configuration.
- The Q6 core PLL (Lucid OLE) and core RCG, and on the ADSP the LPASS
  TCM branch clocks. The PLL sequence differs from
  clk_lucid_ole_pll_configure() in its calibration handshake and output
  enable.
- The stop sequences: halt the NoC port, gate the core clocks, and pulse
  the PDC sync reset and the AOSS restart, as qcom_adsp_shutdown() does.
  The ADSP stop also powers down the LPASS core and audio clock domains.
- The CDSP runs in a Linux IOMMU domain, so map the 107 apertures QHEE
  grants it, plus SMEM and the command DB. The ADSP streams stay in bypass.
- TZ is used once: to stop the ADSP image the UEFI firmware leaves
  running, which also lifts the protection of its carveout.

The register names come from the hardware region directory in the DSP
firmware. Where only the block is known, the offset is used as the name.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
